WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.172 --> 00:00:11.130
KELSEY KRIPPAEHNE: Hey.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.023
Welcome.

00:00:12.023 --> 00:00:13.440
In this session,
we're going to be

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.780
talking about applying
Kubernetes app

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.740
development and best practices.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.430
We're going to talk about
what are the best practices,

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.730
and then we're going to give a
demo on how we use Google Cloud

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.890
DevTools to implement them.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.970
First, we'd like to
introduce ourselves.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.130
I'm Kelsey Krippaehne.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.920
I'm a developer relations
engineer at Google.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.590
ABBY CAREY: And I'm Abby Carey,
developer advocate for Google

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.840
Cloud's developer experiences.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.290
KELSEY KRIPPAEHNE: So as
with any type of development,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.050
following best practices
when working with Kubernetes

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.830
can save you time and
unnecessary frustration.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.200
You may have to write lots
of YAML configuration files.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.990
So unless you're a
YAML expert, you'll

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.150
want to have authoring
support so that you don't

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:58.020
have to constantly look up
what property takes what fields

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.680
or memorize the basic
components of a service.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.510
For local Kubernetes
development,

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.450
you don't need to replicate
your production cluster.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.090
But it's important to have
a cluster for local dev

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.340
that's configured in
a way that ensures

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.380
that what you're
building will be

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.405
compatible with the
production cluster.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.300
Using commands like
kubectl logs to inspect

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.900
pods and services works
OK, but trying to parse

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.740
logs this way can be a pain,
especially for beginners.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.430
A tailored logging interface
is a more efficient way

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.470
to get the info
you're looking for.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.430
Debugging Kubernetes with
CLI commands is cumbersome.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.890
Once again, using a
tool that's tailored

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.930
for Kubernetes
debugging can save you

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.940
from a lot of frustration.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.190
Excessive time to
build and deploy--

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.700
it's always a best practice to
improve development velocity

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.520
and not get stuck in
a lengthy build cycle

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.980
on every change
to the code base.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.840
And finally, it's
a best practice

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.730
to share best practices.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.850
If your team has specific
policies for developing

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.160
Kubernetes apps,
those patterns should

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.650
be accessible as
project templates

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.070
that your team can reuse
to ensure conformity

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:14.640
and to make it easy for
new devs to onboard.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.860
So we're going to start
implementing these best

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.380
practices with
Google's Cloud Code.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.950
Cloud Code is an IDE plugin
that automates dev workflows

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.050
for cloud-native applications.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.750
You can see Cloud Code
as sort of a bridge

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:32.040
between you developing in
your IDE and Google Cloud.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.010
It's bringing the cloud to you.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:36.870
Cloud Code is
particularly helpful

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.210
when developing with
Kubernetes because it

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.930
comes pre-installed with
tools like Skaffold, minikube,

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.640
and the Cloud SDK.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.250
Skaffold is a command
line tool that

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.710
handles the workflow
for building, pushing,

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:52.230
and deploying your application.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.870
Cloud Code uses Skaffold
behind the scenes,

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:59.610
so it acts as sort of a user
interface for the Skaffold CLI.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.250
minikube sets up a local
Kubernetes cluster.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:07.120
It's small but powerful and
extremely beginner-friendly.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.020
Cloud Code is available for
VS Code and JetBrains IDEs,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.160
like IntelliJ.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.060
You can also try out Cloud
Code on Google's web-based IDE,

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.100
the Cloud Shell Editor.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.270
All of these tools--

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:22.240
Cloud Code, Cloud Shell,
Skaffold, and minikube--

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.210
are 100% free to use.

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:28.390
And we encourage you, if you
want to try it out right now,

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:31.060
go ahead and install
Cloud Code on your IDE

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.190
or try it out in
your web browser

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:37.480
with this link to an
interactive tutorial.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.910
So we've talked
about best practices

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.170
and how it can be difficult
to implement them.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.970
The question now is, how do
these Google Cloud DevTools

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:50.020
make it easy to incorporate best
practices into your Kubernetes

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.030
development workflow?

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:55.030
First, you don't need to
be a YAML expert in order

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:58.150
to write functional
Kubernetes resource manifests.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:01.270
Tools like Cloud Code
that provide YAML snippets

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.880
and autocompletes make
it so that you can author

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.760
entire Kubernetes
manifests without looking

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.130
at YAML documentation.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:11.110
You should be developing
on a cluster that

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.390
replicates your production
cluster as much as

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.990
possible to avoid issues
when pushing to production.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:19.570
You can configure
a minikube cluster

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:23.350
to quickly develop and test
locally with no added cost.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.875
You can also use Skaffold
to create distinct profiles

00:04:26.875 --> 00:04:30.070
for dev versus prod
and incorporate

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:33.580
customized [INAUDIBLE] for
even more powerful deployment

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.050
workflows.

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.030
As mentioned before,
it's a good idea

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:40.180
to use a dedicated logging
tool that makes Kubernetes

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.890
logs more accessible.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:44.110
With Cloud Code's
new logging support,

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:47.770
you can drill down into your
containers and container builds

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.210
and your deployment logs.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:52.660
And you'll see that in
a minute in the demo.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.630
For debugging, sometimes
prints and log statements

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:57.040
just aren't enough.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.680
It's useful to be able
to step through the code.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.550
Cloud Code brings Kubernetes
debugging into your IDE.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.760
It lets you do
step-by-step debugging live

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.000
in your Kubernetes cluster,
locally and remotely,

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.150
using the IDE's debugging
interface that you're already

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.590
familiar with.

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.780
Having an automated
local dev loop

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:18.610
is a general best
practice, and you

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:22.720
can setup Kubernetes iterative
development using Skaffold.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.330
Skaffold has a file
sync functionality

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:28.420
that prevents you from needing
to rebuild and redeploy

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:31.240
your containers on
every little change.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.540
And finally, you can use a
tool like Cloud Code's custom

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.150
samples to create
project templates

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:41.140
or samples to easily distribute
your team's best practices.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.740
With custom samples, you can
have a reusable Kubernetes

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.410
configured app that your
team can access right

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:48.910
from their IDE.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:52.505
And now I'll hand things
over to Abby for a demo.

00:05:52.505 --> 00:05:53.630
ABBY CAREY: Thanks, Kelsey.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:56.120
Let's get into IntelliJ
and see some of these best

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.040
practices in action.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.990
To kick off this
demo, I'll show how

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:02.420
you can Kubernetify
a non Kubernetes

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.190
application with Skaffold.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:06.620
I already have Cloud Code
installed from the JetBrains

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.690
marketplace, which
means Skaffold

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:11.900
has been installed, too, since
it's a managed dependency.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.410
I'll use Skaffold's init
--generate-manifests command

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:18.260
to get this app working
with Kubernetes in seconds.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:21.020
And I want to mention that
this functionality will soon

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:22.880
be added to Cloud Code's UI.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:24.980
You can view Cloud
Code as a frontend

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:27.710
for Skaffold, which is
a command-line tool.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:31.460
And now Skaffold's going to
prompt me to pick my builder.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:35.210
And for my frontend, I will
be building with Buildpacks.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:38.270
And for my backend, I'll use
the existing Dockerfile here.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:40.760
Skaffold looks at my
application and determines

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.080
what the Kubernetes
resources are,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.560
and then it creates a
deployment.yaml file

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.150
for each one.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:49.160
And now that that's
finished, I have

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.770
manifests generated
for my application,

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:54.800
and a skaffold.yaml
file that specifies

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.140
it's a build-and-deployment
pipeline.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.590
We'll take a closer look
at skaffold.yaml later.

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:03.290
This application is
ready for Kubernetes,

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:07.490
but these manifests that were
generated are starting points.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.920
You will likely want
to customize them.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.740
Your organization may have
certain naming patterns

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:14.640
or file system layouts.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.760
In this case, this
application needs

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.950
some environment
variables and labels

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:21.290
for the frontend and
backend containers,

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:24.050
and an additional
Kubernetes resource

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:27.740
to deploy a MongoDB
service for our database.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:29.810
I've added a version
of this application

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:34.400
with these changes made to a
Custom Samples Git repository.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.590
Cloud Code's Custom
Samples feature

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:39.200
allows your organization
to easily distribute

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.750
best practices by letting
developers connect

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.440
their IDEs to custom sample
repos, which I've already

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.520
done here.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:49.610
A team best practice is to
have starter projects that

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.880
are setup to fit your team's
preferred configurations.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:55.760
When talking in the
context of K8s development,

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.800
this would include
run configurations

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.690
and certain YAML files.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:02.780
And my new ready-to-go
sample is called Nextbook,

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:03.800
so I'll create it.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:11.090
The Nextbook application
is a basic Kubernetes app

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:13.490
with a frontend and
backend service.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:16.700
We've kept this demo app
simple to better demonstrate

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:18.950
complex Kubernetes dev flows.

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:22.520
It includes logic to read
from and write to a database,

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:26.430
and displays the most
recently added database entry.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:29.090
Let's take a look at the
contents of this project.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:30.830
This project is
configured to use

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:34.850
Skaffold for both development
and production workflows.

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:38.030
That's all setup with
this skaffold.yaml file.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:41.330
I've also set up
runConfigurations in my .idea

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:42.080
folder.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.660
By committing these files
to your project's repo,

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:47.540
this enables any developer
to run and develop

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:49.310
your application the same way.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.220
This helps with onboarding
in a team environment.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:54.980
skaffold.yaml ties
the container images

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:57.680
we're going to build
to the K8s resources

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.330
we're going to deploy.

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:02.270
Skaffold supports a number
of container image builders,

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:05.750
like Docker, Jib, Ko, and more.

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:07.520
I've already setup
my Skaffold file

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.700
to build my backend with a
Dockerfile and my frontend

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:12.650
image with buildpacks.

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:15.560
Buildpacks are a set of
opinionated image builders

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:18.320
that encode language-specific
best practices

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.570
for containerizing applications.

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:24.380
They save you from having
to write custom Dockerfiles.

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:27.710
Skaffold profiles are very
powerful for separating

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:31.200
configurations for different
uses or environments,

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.510
including build, test, and
deployment configurations.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:39.690
I have two already set for
development and production,

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.660
which is a best practice.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:44.720
My dev profile applies
development settings,

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:46.920
which shouldn't be
enabled in production.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:49.580
One of our new products,
Google Cloud Deploy,

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:53.330
leverages Skaffold profiles to
gather configuration details

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:55.670
for each target environment.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:57.260
I'll dig deeper into
the Skaffold file

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:59.000
as I go through this demo.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:01.880
Cloud Code automatically
installs necessary tools,

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:03.860
like Skaffold, so
we're ready to start

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:06.020
working on this application.

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:09.560
Cloud Code comes with
a Kubernetes Explorer.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:13.010
This gives you a view of all
your deployed K8s resources

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:16.240
and is an excellent
tool for operators.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:18.640
Looking closer at one
of my existing clusters

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:23.750
I've deployed to, I can
explore my deployed resources,

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:28.055
and even shell into a running
pod to run some commands.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:35.210
Cloud Code supports any
conformant Kubernetes cluster,

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:38.270
whether that's a local
cluster, like minikube or k3d,

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:41.720
or hosted by a cloud provider.

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:44.450
Today, I'll be deploying
this application

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:48.290
to my GKE dev cluster.

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:50.570
For this sample app,
my run configurations

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:53.210
are already defined
so that any developer

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:56.740
can run this application on
IntelliJ the same way I would.

00:10:56.740 --> 00:11:01.470
I have two configurations--
development and production.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.800
It's a best practice
to tailor workflows

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.510
for different contexts.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:08.270
Let's take a look at my
development configuration.

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:11.150
This is a Cloud Code
Kubernetes configuration

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:12.320
that I've built on.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.140
My container register
URL is already set,

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:19.070
and this is needed because my
skaffold.yaml doesn't specify

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:22.460
an image repository, allowing
developers to easily deploy

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:23.960
to multiple locations.

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:27.470
An example of this is if you
have a personal dev repository

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:29.870
and a production repository.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:34.400
My Run configuration is set
to run on my GKE dev cluster.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:37.940
The option to automatically
rebuild and rerun

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.660
turns on Skaffold's watch mode
for iterative development.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:43.940
In the Build-Deploy
section, we already

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:46.820
have a Skaffold file, so
nothing needs to be done here.

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:50.360
As a side note, though, if you
don't have a Skaffold file,

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:53.780
this is the spot where you'd
be prompted to initialize one

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:55.760
with just a couple clicks.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.160
One last thing I want to
touch on before running

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:02.540
is that I have a Deployment
profile set named dev.

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:04.760
dev is one of my
Skaffold profiles

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:08.660
that I already defined
in skaffold.yaml.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.970
Looking at my
skaffold.yaml file again,

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:15.920
my dev profile specifies
a Dockerfile target, dev,

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.590
for my backend Dockerfile.

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:22.300
Now taking a look at
my backend Dockerfile,

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.060
you'll see that
it's multi-staged.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:27.390
With my dev target set,
running my configuration

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:31.290
will build the base image,
along with the dev portion here,

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:34.960
turning on my Flask
backend's debug mode.

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:37.570
And if the target
were set to prod,

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:40.210
which is for my production
run configuration,

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:45.070
my Flask debugger would be
turned off in production mode.

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:48.430
Now I'll focus on my dev cluster
in the Kubernetes Explorer

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:51.430
and run this with my
development configuration.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:56.310
Right now, Cloud Code
is building and pushing

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:59.550
my container images
to my cluster.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:01.920
Now that that's done,
Cloud Code port forwards

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:04.630
my cluster's services
to my local machine,

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:07.380
so all I have to do is click
the link for my frontend.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:09.780
You can also explicitly
set up port forwarding

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.960
for other resources, like
deployments or stateful sets

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:14.130
in skaffold.yaml.

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:16.080
Now let's take a look
at this application.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:18.150
And this is my
application running

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.580
remotely on my GKE cluster.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:22.600
Everything is
looking as expected,

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:25.110
so I'll enter a new
entry to the database.

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:32.040
And yep, my output box is
displaying the latest entry.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:35.730
Deploying an app to a cluster
generates a ton of logs.

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:39.630
Loading container images,
deployments, spinning up pods,

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:43.470
and the logs streaming from your
containers all generate logs.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:45.840
Figuring out the best way
to view all these logs

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:49.170
can require running
multiple kubectl commands.

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:52.320
Cloud Code splits the logs
up into easily selectable

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:53.430
sections.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:56.010
This makes finding where errors
occurred in your deployment

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:57.900
process easy.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.360
Logs are also streamed
for each container,

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:04.390
letting you focus on different
components of your application.

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:07.140
So right now we're still
running our application,

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:12.130
and Skaffold's watch mode is
waiting for us to make changes.

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:14.880
Now let's look at the YAML
of our deployed application

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:16.680
and see if we can
make it better follow

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:19.430
Kubernetes best practices.

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:22.030
We don't have a readiness
probe, and you should always

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:24.640
have one so that Kubernetes
knows when your app is

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:26.230
ready to serve traffic.

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:28.270
This gets us a
step closer towards

00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:31.900
zero-downtime deployments
and higher availability.

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:34.360
We're going to use Cloud
Code's YAML authoring

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:37.060
support to create
this probe real quick.

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:40.960
I'll go down to my container
and press Control-Space.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.390
And there's the option
for a readinessProbe.

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:45.550
Now to add some parameters.

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:49.720
I'll press Control-Space
again and pick tcpSocket.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:52.760
My port is 8080.

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:55.550
What other parameters
do I need, though?

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:59.840
Hovering over readinessProbe
shows me documentation,

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:03.410
and I see that I have
a couple more options.

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:07.670
Now I'll add
initialDelaySeconds, set to 5,

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.820
and periodSeconds, set to 5.

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:12.320
That's looking good.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:14.870
Now I'll save this file.

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:17.010
Skaffold's watch
mode just kicked off

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:20.190
and we're redeploying.

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:21.510
And now it's done.

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:24.030
Our readiness probe is ready.

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:26.160
This whole rebuilding
and redeploying process

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:29.010
is pretty fast, but
we can make it faster,

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:31.170
and that is with file sync.

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:33.600
By setting up Skaffold's
file sync feature,

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.540
you can copy over a local
file that you've just

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:40.140
edited with one in your
deployed container in seconds.

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:43.710
This avoids the need to
rebuild, redeploy, and restart

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:46.080
your corresponding
pod on a file change,

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.480
saving you local
development time.

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:51.570
To get file sync working
with my backend's Dockerfile,

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:53.910
a little extra
configuration is needed.

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:57.270
But to save a little time,
I've already done that.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:16:01.380
I'm using inferred syncing when
using my dev Skaffold profile.

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:04.020
This means that Skaffold
will look at my Dockerfile

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:07.320
to determine the location of
where my edited files should

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:08.370
be synced to.

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:11.280
All you need to do is tell
Skaffold what files to watch.

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:16.170
I've added *.py so that any
Python file in my backend that

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:17.850
I edit will trigger a sync.

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:20.010
My frontend automatically
has file sync

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:22.650
turned on, because it's
being built with buildpacks.

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:26.190
This autosync feature is
specific to buildpacks.

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:28.770
There are file type limitations
when using buildpacks,

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:31.650
but JavaScript is supported,
so my frontend syncing

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:32.580
is good to go.

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:35.580
File sync may require
configuring your web framework

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:38.280
to detect and respond
to file changes.

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:41.160
I'm using Flask's development
mode to restart the app

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:42.540
on a .py change.

00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:44.580
For compiled languages
or frameworks

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:46.530
that don't provide
this support, you'll

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:48.690
need to use an
external file watcher,

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:51.120
like Nodemon or Watchexec.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:52.710
If you're building
with buildpacks,

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:55.330
Watchexec is
automatically configured.

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:59.100
Now let's see if we can
sync a backend Python file.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:02.070
To visually test this,
I'll hard code a new entry

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:06.240
into the output text box
that says, This is syncing.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:12.290
Now I'll open my Run
window back up and save it.

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:14.119
My logs are showing
that a sync just

00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:18.440
finished, so let's check out my
Kubernetes app now and give it

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:20.089
a refresh.

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:22.579
And there is that data
I just set in there.

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:25.400
With Skaffold's file
syncing, locally iterating

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:29.870
on a deployed Kubernetes
application is super fast.

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:31.640
Now let's talk about debugging.

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:35.000
There are a ton of ways to
debug a Kubernetes application.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:37.850
But what if debugging it
was as simple as debugging

00:17:37.850 --> 00:17:40.100
any application in your IDE?

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:42.350
You should run and
debug your applications

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:44.940
in environments that
closely mimic production,

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:48.290
so why not remotely
on a GKE cluster?

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:50.840
I could also switch to
using a local minikube

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:52.760
cluster to cut down on latency.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:55.940
But since we're already
talking about GKE clusters,

00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:57.120
I'll stick with that.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:17:59.150
So now I'm going
to try and debug

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:02.420
my frontend's app.js file.

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:05.840
And all I should need to
do is place a breakpoint.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:09.500
I'll set it to trigger
after I post a message.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:13.040
And now I'll kick off debugging
with Cloud Code's debug mode.

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:19.810
And once I've set my paths
for my frontend and backend

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:23.350
containers, I can now
open up my application

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:26.650
and post a message to see if
we can trigger this breakpoint.

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:33.270
And there we go.

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:37.290
I'm now remotely debugging my
application on my GKE cluster

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:39.210
right from my IDE.

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:42.420
I'll just step through
the code a couple times

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:45.340
and then finish running it.

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:46.075
Super simple.

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:52.750
It's a good practice to add
tests for your container image,

00:18:52.750 --> 00:18:55.120
and Skaffold supports
running tests.

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:56.950
For the last part
of this demo, I'm

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:59.740
going to run container
structure tests with Skaffold.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:02.350
Container structure tests
validate the structure

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:05.830
of your container image,
can check command outputs,

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:09.040
and verify file
content and metadata.

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:12.130
Here we have test files for
the backend and frontend

00:19:12.130 --> 00:19:13.330
containers.

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:17.260
These files are written in
YAML, but you can also use JSON.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:20.320
In the backend, I'm checking
that Python is installed

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:23.290
and that the requirements
file exists in the container.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:25.240
In the frontend,
I'm testing to make

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:28.540
sure I don't have any
comments that include xxx left

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:30.460
in my code, because I
definitely don't want

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:32.150
to push those into production.

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:34.090
So my tests are written.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:36.730
Now let's go over
to skaffold.yaml.

00:19:36.730 --> 00:19:40.360
In my prod profile,
I've configured Skaffold

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:42.910
to run the container tests.

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:45.790
Now after Skaffold
builds the image,

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:49.090
it'll run the tests before
deploying to the cluster.

00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:51.700
I'll switch to my
production run configuration

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:53.245
and see if these tests pass.

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:02.090
Looks like one of my
tests didn't pass.

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:04.420
Let's take a closer look.

00:20:04.420 --> 00:20:08.540
So my backend
container test passed,

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:11.150
but it looks like my frontend
container has a comment that's

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:12.380
causing this to fail.

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:13.730
I'll go fix that right now.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:22.490
I'll delete this comment
here and give it a run

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:23.270
one more time.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.130
And everything passed this time.

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:36.530
This application is now
ready for production,

00:20:36.530 --> 00:20:38.480
thanks to Google
Cloud's dev tools,

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:39.800
like Cloud Code and Skaffold.

